1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,775
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,775 --> 00:00:06,680
This is chapter thirteen: pattern matching.

3
00:00:07,130 --> 00:00:15,359
It arrived in three instalments: instanceof patterns in Java 16, then switch patterns and record patterns in 21.

4
00:00:15,359 --> 00:00:20,355
It only becomes compelling when all three combine with sealed types.

5
00:00:20,355 --> 00:00:26,159
Then Java can dispatch on shape, destructure, and prove you handled every case.

6
00:00:27,059 --> 00:00:29,443
Here's an event handler in Java 8.

7
00:00:29,443 --> 00:00:31,966
Test the type, cast it, then use it.

8
00:00:31,966 --> 00:00:34,910
The type is written twice in every branch.

9
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:37,158
Nothing checks completeness.

10
00:00:37,158 --> 00:00:41,909
Add a fifth event type and this still compiles, then throws in production.

11
00:00:42,359 --> 00:00:46,370
Getting at the event's fields needs a separate step after the cast.

12
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:51,838
And that else throw at the bottom is a confession that the compiler couldn't help.

13
00:00:51,838 --> 00:00:53,796
Pattern matching fixes all four.

14
00:00:54,696 --> 00:00:57,111
A pattern is a test plus a binding.

15
00:00:57,111 --> 00:01:01,873
It asks, does this value have this shape, and if so, names the parts.

16
00:01:01,873 --> 00:01:04,979
A type pattern binds the value with its type.

17
00:01:05,429 --> 00:01:09,887
A record pattern goes further and binds the record's components directly.

18
00:01:10,337 --> 00:01:17,484
And patterns nest, so you can bind the components of components, using underscore for parts you don't need.

19
00:01:17,934 --> 00:01:19,651
Remember the two pillars.

20
00:01:19,651 --> 00:01:22,193
Sealed types give you exhaustiveness.

21
00:01:22,193 --> 00:01:24,323
Records give you destructuring.

22
00:01:24,323 --> 00:01:28,857
Without sealed types, pattern matching is just a nicer instanceof.

23
00:01:29,757 --> 00:01:32,245
Here's the same handler in Java 21.

24
00:01:32,245 --> 00:01:39,922
The events are a sealed interface of records, and each case destructures its record directly into variables.

25
00:01:39,922 --> 00:01:48,381
The guard, when retry, handles the retryable failures, at the same level as every other branch, instead of a nested if.

26
00:01:48,831 --> 00:01:51,584
No casts, no else throw, and no default.

27
00:01:51,584 --> 00:01:54,543
The compiler proves the switch is complete.

28
00:01:54,543 --> 00:02:01,012
Add a fifth event type and this method stops compiling, along with every other switch over it.

29
00:02:01,912 --> 00:02:08,800
Cases are tested in order, so guarded patterns come before the unguarded pattern for the same type.

30
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:15,340
And when one label lists several patterns, you can't name bindings, since only one will match.

31
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:17,357
Use underscore, from Java 22.

32
00:02:17,807 --> 00:02:25,048
Get the order wrong and the compiler rejects it: this case label is dominated by a preceding case label.

33
00:02:25,048 --> 00:02:30,269
In an if-else chain, that unreachable branch would just silently never run.

34
00:02:30,269 --> 00:02:32,079
Here it's a compile error.

35
00:02:32,979 --> 00:02:33,965
Patterns nest.

36
00:02:33,965 --> 00:02:36,712
Here, a payment result contains a card.

37
00:02:36,712 --> 00:02:44,670
The first case matches only approved payments on a Visa card, and binds the last four digits, all in one pattern.

38
00:02:44,670 --> 00:02:49,811
The last case uses underscore for the card network it doesn't care about.

39
00:02:50,261 --> 00:02:52,765
That's powerful, but keep it shallow.

40
00:02:52,765 --> 00:02:57,365
Past about two levels of nesting, a pattern becomes write-only code.

41
00:02:58,265 --> 00:03:02,199
In everyday code, the instanceof pattern is the big win.

42
00:03:02,199 --> 00:03:10,489
An equals method becomes one line, with no separate cast and no null check, because null never matches a type pattern.

43
00:03:10,939 --> 00:03:14,779
The binding follows flow scoping, not normal block scoping.

44
00:03:14,779 --> 00:03:19,073
It's in scope wherever the compiler can prove the pattern matched.

45
00:03:19,073 --> 00:03:24,735
So with a negated test and an early throw, cart is in scope for the rest of the method.

46
00:03:24,735 --> 00:03:26,882
That makes guard clauses natural.

47
00:03:26,882 --> 00:03:34,235
But with an or, the right side runs when the pattern didn't match, so using the binding there is a compile error.

48
00:03:35,135 --> 00:03:37,485
Null handling is always explicit.

49
00:03:37,485 --> 00:03:41,471
instanceof with a pattern simply returns false for null.

50
00:03:41,921 --> 00:03:48,385
A pattern switch with no case null throws a NullPointerException, just like the old switch did.

51
00:03:48,835 --> 00:03:51,651
Write case null to handle it in its own branch.

52
00:03:52,101 --> 00:03:56,073
Or case null comma default to treat it like anything else.

53
00:03:56,073 --> 00:03:57,717
Either way, you decided.

54
00:03:57,717 --> 00:03:58,949
Nothing is silent.

55
00:03:59,849 --> 00:04:02,455
Pattern matching isn't always the answer.

56
00:04:02,455 --> 00:04:12,116
If every branch does the same kind of thing with each type's own data, like computing a fee, that's a dispatch table for behaviour the types should own.

57
00:04:12,566 --> 00:04:15,138
An interface method is better there.

58
00:04:15,138 --> 00:04:18,710
New pricing rules need no change to existing code.

59
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:21,961
This is the classic expression problem.

60
00:04:21,961 --> 00:04:24,906
Polymorphism makes adding new types easy.

61
00:04:24,906 --> 00:04:32,807
Pattern matching makes adding new operations easy, like rendering, serialising or reporting over a closed set.

62
00:04:32,807 --> 00:04:36,973
Choose based on which one actually changes in your domain.

63
00:04:37,873 --> 00:04:42,629
Under the hood, a pattern switch compiles to an invokedynamic call.

64
00:04:42,629 --> 00:04:46,818
A bootstrap builds an efficient matcher once and caches it.

65
00:04:46,818 --> 00:04:50,012
It's not a linear chain of instanceof checks.

66
00:04:50,462 --> 00:04:54,063
Record patterns bind by calling the accessor methods.

67
00:04:54,063 --> 00:04:57,731
So an accessor with side effects runs during matching.

68
00:04:57,731 --> 00:04:59,294
Keep accessors trivial.

69
00:04:59,744 --> 00:05:04,719
And the compiler still adds a hidden default that throws a MatchException.

70
00:05:04,719 --> 00:05:12,920
If a library adds a new subtype and your switch isn't recompiled, you get a loud failure instead of a silent wrong answer.

71
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:18,432
That's why sealing a public library type makes adding a subtype a breaking change.

72
00:05:19,332 --> 00:05:20,550
Production rules.

73
00:05:20,550 --> 00:05:25,852
Use instanceof patterns in equals, and the negated form for guard clauses.

74
00:05:25,852 --> 00:05:28,431
Pair sealed interfaces with records.

75
00:05:28,431 --> 00:05:31,082
Leave default out of sealed switches.

76
00:05:31,082 --> 00:05:32,801
Put guarded cases first.

77
00:05:32,801 --> 00:05:37,028
Use var in record patterns and underscore for unused parts.

78
00:05:37,028 --> 00:05:40,324
And write case null wherever null is possible.

79
00:05:40,774 --> 00:05:44,256
Never use pattern matching where polymorphism fits.

80
00:05:44,256 --> 00:05:47,464
Never add default to a switch you want checked.

81
00:05:47,464 --> 00:05:50,058
Don't nest more than about two levels.

82
00:05:50,058 --> 00:05:52,448
Keep side effects out of accessors.

83
00:05:52,448 --> 00:05:55,383
Don't assume a pattern switch handles null.

84
00:05:55,383 --> 00:05:58,523
And don't seal a public library type casually.

85
00:05:59,423 --> 00:06:04,117
Java 16 finalised instanceof patterns, along with records.

86
00:06:04,567 --> 00:06:07,489
Java 17 finalised sealed classes.

87
00:06:07,939 --> 00:06:13,891
Java 21 delivered the complete feature: pattern matching for switch, and record patterns.

88
00:06:14,341 --> 00:06:19,355
Java 22 added the underscore for unnamed variables and patterns.

89
00:06:19,805 --> 00:06:24,841
And patterns over primitive types, like case int i, are still in preview.

90
00:06:24,841 --> 00:06:27,600
They need enable-preview and may change.

91
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:30,429
Everything else in this chapter is final.

92
00:06:31,329 --> 00:06:31,936
To recap.

93
00:06:31,936 --> 00:06:34,300
A pattern is a test plus a binding.

94
00:06:34,300 --> 00:06:40,378
With sealed types and records, you get exhaustive switches that destructure as they match.

95
00:06:40,378 --> 00:06:45,579
Put guarded cases first, and the compiler will reject any that are dominated.

96
00:06:45,579 --> 00:06:50,104
Bindings follow flow scoping, and null handling is always explicit.

97
00:06:50,104 --> 00:06:55,777
And use polymorphism when the types change, pattern matching when the operations do.

98
00:06:56,227 --> 00:07:01,529
Next is chapter fourteen, the language features of Java 21 to 26.

99
00:07:01,529 --> 00:07:03,160
Thanks for watching.
